04-Networking-Protocols

1. TCP — Transmission Control Protocol

Definition

TCP is a connection-oriented and reliable transport-layer protocol used 
to deliver data between devices. It ensures that data reaches the destination correctly, completely, and in the proper order.

Key Features
Connection-oriented
Reliable delivery
Ordered data delivery
Error detection and retransmission
Flow control
Congestion control
Port

TCP itself does not have one fixed port. Applications using TCP use specific ports, such as:

HTTP → 80
HTTPS → 443
SSH → 22
FTP → 21
Interview Answer

TCP is a connection-oriented and reliable transport-layer protocol.
It establishes a connection before transferring data and provides reliable, 
ordered delivery using acknowledgments, sequence numbers, retransmission, flow control, and congestion control.

2. UDP — User Datagram Protocol
Definition

UDP is a connectionless transport-layer protocol that sends data without establishing a connection first.

Key Features
Connectionless
Faster than TCP
Low overhead
No guarantee of delivery
No guarantee of order
No retransmission mechanism
Common Uses
DNS
DHCP
VoIP
Online gaming
Video/audio streaming
Interview Answer

UDP is a connectionless transport-layer protocol that provides fast
data transmission with low overhead. Unlike TCP, it does not guarantee
delivery, ordering, or retransmission, so it is preferred where speed and low latency are more important than reliability.

3. HTTP — Hypertext Transfer Protocol
Definition

HTTP is an application-layer protocol used to transfer web resources between a client and a web server.

Port

80/TCP

How It Works
Client sends an HTTP request.
Web server processes the request.
Server sends an HTTP response.
Browser displays the requested content.
Example

When you access:

http://example.com

the browser communicates with the web server using HTTP.

4. HTTPS — Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure
Definition

HTTPS is HTTP combined with TLS encryption to secure communication between a client and server.

Port

443/TCP

Provides
Encryption
Data integrity
Server authentication
Interview Answer

HTTPS is the secure version of HTTP. It uses TLS to encrypt communication 
between the client and server, helping protect sensitive information such as passwords, payment details, and session data.

5. FTP — File Transfer Protocol
Definition

FTP is an application-layer protocol used to transfer files between a client and server.

Ports
21/TCP → Control connection
20/TCP → Traditionally associated with data transfer in active mode
Features
Upload files
Download files
Manage files/directories
Authentication
Security

Traditional FTP sends credentials and data without encryption, 
so it is not recommended for secure file transfer.

6. SFTP — SSH File Transfer Protocol
Definition

SFTP is a secure file-transfer protocol that operates over SSH.

Port

22/TCP

Features
Encrypted communication
Secure authentication
File upload/download
File management
FTP vs SFTP
Feature	FTP	SFTP
Security	Not encrypted by default	Encrypted
Port	21	22
Protocol basis	FTP	SSH
Secure file transfer	No	Yes
Interview Answer

SFTP is a secure file-transfer protocol that runs over SSH.
It encrypts both authentication information and file data, making it more secure than traditional FTP.

7. SSH — Secure Shell
Definition

SSH is an application-layer protocol used to securely access and manage remote systems.

Port

22/TCP

Common Uses
Remote server administration
Secure command-line access
Secure file transfer through SFTP
Port forwarding
Secure tunneling
Interview Answer

SSH is a secure remote-access protocol that allows administrators to connect 
to and manage remote systems over an encrypted connection. It commonly uses TCP port 22.

8. SMTP — Simple Mail Transfer Protocol
Definition

SMTP is an application-layer protocol used primarily for sending and relaying email.

Common Ports
Port	Purpose
25	SMTP relay
587	Mail submission
465	SMTP over implicit TLS
Important Point

SMTP is mainly used for sending email, not retrieving it.

Interview Answer

SMTP is an application-layer protocol used to send and relay emails from clients to mail servers and between mail servers.

9. POP3 — Post Office Protocol Version 3
Definition

POP3 is an email retrieval protocol used to download emails from a mail server to a client.

Ports
110/TCP → POP3
995/TCP → POP3 over TLS
Key Point

POP3 traditionally focuses on downloading messages to the client and may 
remove them from the server depending on configuration.

Interview Answer

POP3 is an email retrieval protocol that downloads emails from a mail server to the user's device.
It is generally simpler than IMAP and is more suitable when email is primarily accessed from one device.

10. IMAP — Internet Message Access Protocol
Definition

IMAP is an email protocol that allows users to access and manage emails while keeping them stored on the mail server.

Ports
143/TCP → IMAP
993/TCP → IMAP over TLS
Key Features
Server-side email storage
Synchronization across devices
Folders
Read/unread status synchronization
POP3 vs IMAP
Feature	POP3	IMAP
Main purpose	Download emails	Synchronize/access emails
Server storage	Limited/traditional download model	Yes
Multiple devices	Less suitable	Better
Synchronization	Limited	Strong
Interview Answer

IMAP allows users to access and synchronize emails directly from the mail server.
It is useful when the same mailbox needs to be accessed from multiple devices because folders,
read status, and messages can remain synchronized.

11. DNS — Domain Name System
Definition

DNS translates domain names into IP addresses so users can access
services using names instead of numerical IP addresses.

Port

53

Usually:

UDP 53 → normal DNS queries
TCP 53 → situations such as zone transfers and some larger/other DNS operations
Example

www.example.com

↓

DNS lookup

↓

IP address

Common DNS Records
A → IPv4 address
AAAA → IPv6 address
CNAME → Alias
MX → Mail server
NS → Name server
TXT → Text information
Interview Answer

DNS is a name-resolution system that translates domain names into IP addresses. 
It allows users to access websites and services using human-readable names instead of remembering IP addresses.

12. DHCP — Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol
Definition

DHCP automatically provides network configuration information to devices.

It can provide:

IP address
Subnet mask
Default gateway
DNS server
Ports
67/UDP → DHCP server
68/UDP → DHCP client
DORA Process

D — Discover
Client searches for a DHCP server.

O — Offer
Server offers an IP configuration.

R — Request
Client requests the offered configuration.

A — Acknowledgment
Server confirms the IP assignment.

Interview Answer

DHCP automatically assigns IP configuration to network devices.
The basic DHCP process is DORA—Discover, Offer, Request, and Acknowledgment.
It reduces manual configuration and helps prevent IP-address conflicts.

13. ARP — Address Resolution Protocol
Definition

ARP is used in IPv4 networks to determine the MAC address associated with an IP address on the local network.

How It Works

A device knows:

Destination IP

but needs:

Destination MAC

It sends an ARP Request asking which device owns that IP address.

The destination device responds with its MAC address.

The mapping can then be stored in the ARP cache.

Layer

Generally associated with the Network/Internet access boundary rather than fitting neatly into one OSI layer.

Interview Answer

ARP is used in IPv4 networks to resolve an IP address to a MAC address on 
the local network. This allows a device to build the Ethernet frame needed to deliver packets to the correct local destination.

14. ICMP — Internet Control Message Protocol
Definition

ICMP is a network-layer control and diagnostic protocol used to 
communicate network errors and operational information.

Common Uses
Ping
Network troubleshooting
Destination unreachable messages
Time exceeded messages
Example

ping 8.8.8.8

uses ICMP Echo Request and Echo Reply messages.

Interview Answer

ICMP is a network-layer protocol used for error reporting, diagnostics, 
and network-control messages. Tools such as ping use ICMP to test 
whether a destination is reachable and measure response time.

15. SNMP — Simple Network Management Protocol
Definition

SNMP is used to monitor and manage network devices such as routers, switches, servers, printers, and firewalls.

Ports
161/UDP → queries/management
162/UDP → traps/notifications
Components
SNMP Manager → Monitoring/management system
SNMP Agent → Software running on the monitored device
MIB → Defines manageable information
Example

A monitoring system can use SNMP to check:

CPU utilization
Memory usage
Interface status
Network traffic
Device availability
Interview Answer

SNMP is a network-management protocol used to monitor and manage network devices.
An SNMP manager communicates with agents running on devices to collect information 
such as interface status, traffic, CPU, and memory utilization.

16. Telnet
Definition

Telnet is a protocol used for remote command-line access to another system.

Port

23/TCP

Security Problem

Telnet sends communication, including credentials, without encryption.

Therefore, SSH is preferred for secure remote administration.

Telnet vs SSH
Feature	Telnet	SSH
Port	23	22
Encryption	No	Yes
Secure remote access	No	Yes
Modern usage	Limited	Common
Interview Answer

Telnet is a remote-access protocol that provides command-line access
to another system, but it does not encrypt communication. 
Because of this security weakness, SSH is normally used instead of Telnet for remote administration.

17. RDP — Remote Desktop Protocol
Definition

RDP is a Microsoft protocol used to remotely access a Windows graphical desktop.

Port

3389/TCP

RDP can also use UDP for some transport scenarios.

Common Uses
Remote Windows administration
Remote desktop access
Troubleshooting
Working on a remote Windows machine
Interview Answer

RDP is a remote desktop protocol primarily used to access Windows 
systems remotely through a graphical interface. The default TCP
port is 3389, and it is commonly used by administrators for remote system management.

